WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.660
Usually when we conceptualize the future, we rely on this very specific, almost unavoidable spatial metaphor.

00:09.120 --> 00:11.760
We picture a vast, empty room.

00:11.900 --> 00:12.780
Right, like a container.

00:13.020 --> 00:20.060
Exactly. We imagine ourselves standing at the threshold, holding a flashlight, you know, and just stepping forward into the dark.

00:20.160 --> 00:25.160
And the underlying assumption there is that the future is just, it's a place that already exists.

00:25.320 --> 00:27.340
Yeah, waiting for us to stumble into it.

00:27.340 --> 00:30.820
Waiting to be illuminated or mapped out. We just have to navigate it.

00:30.900 --> 00:38.400
But we're doing a deep dive today into a new working draft from July 2026, and it completely shatters that container metaphor.

00:38.620 --> 00:42.960
It really does. It's called generative continuation geometry by an author named Flyxion.

00:43.160 --> 00:45.820
And it argues that the future is not a place we just walk into.

00:45.900 --> 00:50.380
It's a structure that is generated, compressed, and measured step by step.

00:50.760 --> 00:53.880
So the mission for you listening today, our third person in this conversation,

00:53.880 --> 00:58.140
is to understand how we stop measuring the future and start, well, growing it.

00:58.280 --> 01:03.200
Growing it, yeah. That transition from measuring to growing is, I mean, it's the hinge of the entire document.

01:03.560 --> 01:09.980
And I want to promise you, the listener, right up front, we are going to be looking at some heavy concepts today.

01:10.320 --> 01:13.660
Things like Lipschitz constants and Hausdorff distances.

01:13.660 --> 01:17.840
But we are absolutely stripping away the jargon.

01:17.980 --> 01:21.100
We're going for pure, mind-expanding, aha moments.

01:21.200 --> 01:21.880
No, absolutely.

01:22.140 --> 01:25.040
I mean, if you look at earlier geometric frameworks,

01:25.560 --> 01:30.660
mathematicians would look at a system, say, a pendulum or an economy or even a chess game,

01:30.740 --> 01:33.300
and ask, like, how much room does this state have?

01:33.400 --> 01:33.760
Right.

01:34.180 --> 01:36.180
How far apart are two possible futures?

01:36.780 --> 01:38.960
If I make a slight change to the chessboard right now,

01:39.080 --> 01:42.800
how wildly do the outcomes diverge, say, 10 moves down the line?

01:42.800 --> 01:44.000
It's very measurement-focused.

01:44.260 --> 01:47.420
Right. It takes the space of all possible futures as a given.

01:47.780 --> 01:49.060
It treats time as a geography.

01:49.240 --> 01:52.760
Like the flashlight in the dark room, we're just measuring the dimensions of the room.

01:53.000 --> 01:58.640
But Flyxion proposes that the room doesn't even exist until the specific rules of the system build it.

01:58.740 --> 02:03.940
Exactly. And to illustrate this, the draft introduces this concept called continuation attractors.

02:04.140 --> 02:08.440
And it contrasts it with a really great biological analogy, the growth of a fern.

02:08.820 --> 02:10.660
The fern analogy was so helpful for me.

02:10.660 --> 02:18.900
Because if you want to digitally store a highly detailed image of a fern, you run into a massive data problem, right?

02:19.120 --> 02:26.960
Massive. Because storing the exact spatial coordinates of every single stem, every leaflet, every sub-leaflet,

02:27.260 --> 02:29.160
it requires an enormous amount of memory.

02:29.400 --> 02:31.040
The complexity is just staggering.

02:31.040 --> 02:39.840
But if you look closely at a fern, one little pinna, like a leaflet off the main stem, is basically just a smaller version of the whole fern.

02:40.040 --> 02:43.540
Right. And a pineal on that leaflet is a smaller fern again. It's fractal.

02:43.680 --> 02:45.600
Yes. It has self-similarity.

02:45.860 --> 02:45.980
Correct.

02:46.000 --> 02:48.700
Which means you don't actually need to store the shape.

02:48.840 --> 02:51.580
No. You only need to store a very small set of growth rules.

02:51.880 --> 02:56.420
You apply those rules to a starting point, take the output, and feed it back into the rules.

02:56.500 --> 02:57.100
Over and over.

02:57.100 --> 03:01.120
Exactly. The shape isn't sitting in a digital container waiting to be loaded.

03:01.540 --> 03:05.540
It is dynamically generated by the recursive application of an operator.

03:05.860 --> 03:11.960
And the distinction the author draws here between true generation and mere accumulation is, it's vital,

03:12.460 --> 03:16.000
they reference Stuart Kaufman's concept of the adjacent possible.

03:16.160 --> 03:17.200
Yeah, that's a key contrast.

03:17.380 --> 03:23.080
Because in Kaufman's model, you look at your current state, find everything you can reach in one step,

03:23.080 --> 03:27.520
and you just add it to your territory. Then you look at that new border, find everything you can read

03:27.520 --> 03:28.860
from there and add that.

03:28.860 --> 03:35.300
It resembles a puddle spreading outward on pavement. The space of possibilities just keeps unioning with

03:35.300 --> 03:38.420
its adjacent possibilities. It grows monotonically.

03:38.560 --> 03:38.780
Right.

03:39.200 --> 03:44.820
And assuming the universe isn't strictly finite, that puddle just spreads without bound. It never settles.

03:44.980 --> 03:51.100
It never forms a coherent, self-similar structure. Flyxion is mathematically strict about the spreading

03:51.100 --> 03:54.880
outward. Endlessly is accumulation. It's just piling things up.

03:54.880 --> 03:56.900
True generation requires something else.

03:57.020 --> 03:59.640
Yeah. It requires a mechanism that forces convergence.

03:59.960 --> 04:03.180
And I'll admit, I found myself struggling with that convergence part at first.

04:03.440 --> 04:09.080
Because if we are endlessly applying rules, you know, feeding the output back into the machine over

04:09.080 --> 04:13.140
and over, my intuition is that the math should just explode into infinity.

04:13.520 --> 04:16.240
Sure. Because piling things up makes sense. It just gets bigger.

04:16.680 --> 04:22.260
Right. But generating a highly specific, intricate shape requires the math to settle down.

04:22.260 --> 04:25.080
It requires, like, a gravitational pull.

04:25.280 --> 04:30.440
And that gravitational pull is provided by a specific type of mathematical rule called a contraction.

04:31.060 --> 04:33.740
And this works through something called the Hutchinson operator.

04:33.940 --> 04:35.680
Okay. Break down a contraction for us.

04:36.040 --> 04:40.620
So, a contraction is a rule that strictly reduces the distance between points.

04:41.080 --> 04:43.620
Imagine laying a map of your city on a table.

04:43.700 --> 04:44.440
Okay. Got it.

04:44.440 --> 04:52.400
Now, imagine a specialized scanner that duplicates that map, shrinks it down to half its size, and drops it back onto the original map.

04:52.740 --> 04:58.440
So, if I pick any two coffee shops on the original map, the distance between them might be, say, two miles.

04:58.700 --> 05:00.160
But on the shrunken copy...

05:00.160 --> 05:03.500
The distance between those exact same two coffee shops is now one mile.

05:03.720 --> 05:04.180
Wow.

05:04.620 --> 05:04.860
Right?

05:05.180 --> 05:08.100
The distance has been multiplied by a factor strictly less than one.

05:08.100 --> 05:09.960
That is the definition of a contraction.

05:10.420 --> 05:12.300
The rules aren't just creating new points.

05:12.700 --> 05:14.560
They are actively compressing the space.

05:15.100 --> 05:21.680
And to measure that compression across entirely different weird shapes, the author uses the Hausdorff distance.

05:22.740 --> 05:26.060
I know I promise no jargon, but this one is cool.

05:26.540 --> 05:35.360
If we want to move past the intuition and into the actual rigorous geometry, we have to understand how Hausdorff measures the gap between two complex features.

05:35.360 --> 05:42.100
Yeah, the Hausdorff distance is brilliant because it handles asymmetrical, messy shapes perfectly.

05:42.880 --> 05:46.520
If you have shape A and shape B, you don't just measure the distance between their centers.

05:46.860 --> 05:48.160
Because that wouldn't tell you much.

05:48.180 --> 05:48.380
Right.

05:48.680 --> 05:50.240
You find the worst case scenario.

05:50.900 --> 05:55.660
You imagine a point in shape A that is as far away as possible from any part of shape B.

05:56.080 --> 05:57.500
And you calculate that distance.

05:57.600 --> 05:57.840
Okay.

05:58.000 --> 05:59.260
Then you do the exact reverse.

05:59.380 --> 05:59.580
Yeah.

05:59.720 --> 06:02.820
You find the point in shape B that is farthest from any part of shape A.

06:02.820 --> 06:06.980
And the larger of those two worst case distances becomes your official Hausdorff distance.

06:07.240 --> 06:07.680
Exactly.

06:08.120 --> 06:14.060
It guarantees that no matter where you are in shape A, you are at most that distance away from shape B, and vice versa.

06:14.380 --> 06:17.600
It is a definitive metric for how well two futures overlap.

06:17.600 --> 06:18.080
Okay.

06:18.300 --> 06:27.080
So, armed with the Hausdorff distance to measure the space and contractions to shrink the space, Flyxion invokes the Banach fixed point theorem.

06:27.080 --> 06:27.480
Yeah.

06:27.660 --> 06:33.840
And this requires the space of all possible shapes to be complete, meaning there are no missing pieces.

06:33.980 --> 06:34.180
Right.

06:34.360 --> 06:35.480
No mathematical potholes.

06:35.600 --> 06:36.060
Exactly.

06:36.360 --> 06:36.580
Yeah.

06:36.680 --> 06:44.860
In a complete metric space, if you have a sequence of shapes that keep getting closer and closer together, meaning their Hausdorff distances are shrinking towards zero,

06:45.920 --> 06:49.020
they won't suddenly vanish or fall through a hole in the mathematics.

06:49.420 --> 06:53.360
They're guaranteed to land on a concrete, mathematically real limit.

06:53.360 --> 06:56.560
And the continuation attractor theorem builds directly on that.

06:56.560 --> 07:07.020
If our growth rules are contractions and our space is complete, applying those rules repeatedly will inevitably drag any starting shape toward one single unique geometric limit.

07:07.340 --> 07:07.960
The attractor.

07:08.280 --> 07:12.080
It doesn't matter if your starting shape was a square, a circle, or just a random scribble.

07:12.420 --> 07:16.280
The contracting rules will pull it relentlessly until it forms the fern.

07:16.360 --> 07:17.060
That's wild.

07:17.060 --> 07:24.760
And once it hits that final shape, applying the rules one more time just gives you the exact same shape back, it has achieved perfect self-similarity.

07:25.080 --> 07:34.040
Okay, let's make this concrete because the draft provides a stunningly stark example of this called the two refusals, which generates the cantor set.

07:34.440 --> 07:36.400
Let's walk through the physical mechanics of it.

07:36.500 --> 07:36.720
Sure.

07:36.720 --> 07:42.640
We take a solid line segment, like a continuous piece of string stretching from zero to one.

07:43.020 --> 07:45.100
Our first rule dictates a refusal.

07:45.740 --> 07:49.980
We have to locate the middle third of that string, cut it out, and discard it.

07:50.220 --> 07:54.420
So you are left with two shorter pieces of string, one on the left, one on the right.

07:54.540 --> 07:54.800
Right.

07:54.900 --> 07:59.140
And our second rule is just to apply the first rule again to whatever survived.

07:59.460 --> 08:01.880
We take the left string, cut out its middle third.

08:02.000 --> 08:03.880
We take the right string, cut out its middle third.

08:04.020 --> 08:06.140
So now we have four even shorter pieces.

08:06.140 --> 08:06.780
Exactly.

08:07.240 --> 08:10.940
And we repeat this recursive refusal out to infinity.

08:11.180 --> 08:14.060
And the mathematics of what remains at the limit of infinity is profound.

08:15.020 --> 08:21.400
You are left with an uncountable number of points, yet the total length of all those points combined is precisely zero.

08:21.780 --> 08:23.560
It is a nowhere dense dust.

08:23.880 --> 08:25.220
A nowhere dense dust.

08:25.740 --> 08:29.600
It's a highly complex, infinitely detailed future geometry.

08:30.200 --> 08:32.820
But it wasn't accumulated by a spreading puddle.

08:32.820 --> 08:37.540
It was entirely determined by two really simple contraction rules.

08:37.540 --> 08:42.600
The instruction set is microscopic, but the resulting reality is infinitely deep.

08:43.160 --> 08:45.720
This completely reframes the scale of analysis.

08:46.180 --> 08:46.760
How so?

08:46.880 --> 08:53.480
Well, the natural unit of study for a complex system is no longer the vast, overwhelming space of the future itself.

08:54.180 --> 08:58.460
The natural unit of study becomes the small set of contracting operations that built it.

08:58.460 --> 09:04.220
And for you listening, applying this to human behavior shifts the paradigm of how we view our personal trajectories.

09:04.540 --> 09:07.560
We so often view our lives as that spreading puddle, right?

09:07.680 --> 09:12.380
A massive accumulation of random events just expanding endlessly into the dark room.

09:12.520 --> 09:12.820
Absolutely.

09:12.820 --> 09:18.440
But the math of the continuation attractor suggests our futures are actually generated by our personal contractions.

09:18.520 --> 09:21.980
Like, if you have a strict rule where you always read for 20 minutes before bed,

09:22.060 --> 09:26.980
or a rule where you never check your phone during a conversation, those aren't just isolated events adding up.

09:27.300 --> 09:28.180
No, they're operators.

09:28.560 --> 09:36.820
If those rules act as true contractions, if they consistently pull your behavior toward a specific center and away from random drift,

09:37.440 --> 09:40.360
they generate a very specific geometric limit.

09:40.500 --> 09:41.740
A behavioral fern.

09:41.740 --> 09:44.280
Yes. Apply daily to their own output.

09:44.680 --> 09:48.860
Those tiny rules generate the precise, fractal shape of your future.

09:49.280 --> 09:51.580
You aren't walking into a pre-existing tomorrow.

09:52.060 --> 09:56.800
You are rendering a cantor set in real time through the rules you refuse to break.

09:57.120 --> 10:00.780
That structural generation covers the first major phase of the draft.

10:01.300 --> 10:04.600
But moving into the second phase, the author flips the perspective entirely.

10:05.060 --> 10:09.780
We transition from examining how futures branch out and grow to how they merge back together.

10:09.780 --> 10:12.480
Right. The author introduces continuation quotients.

10:12.880 --> 10:16.000
We move from the creation of detail to the destruction of distinction.

10:16.640 --> 10:22.840
The draft examines what happens when two entirely different histories produce the exact same reachable future.

10:23.060 --> 10:25.080
And the physical analogy here is just perfect.

10:25.320 --> 10:26.760
Picture two distinct rivers.

10:26.760 --> 10:31.300
One originates from, like, glacial melt on the frozen north face of a mountain range.

10:31.300 --> 10:35.240
It carves through hard granite, dropping down steep, violent waterfalls.

10:35.580 --> 10:43.000
And the other river starts in a warm subterranean spring on the lush south side, meandering lazily through soft limestone.

10:43.000 --> 10:47.940
So their histories, their compositions, their energetic states are completely, entirely different.

10:48.120 --> 10:49.080
Entirely different.

10:49.460 --> 10:52.740
But eventually, geography forces them to a confluence.

10:53.020 --> 10:56.380
They merge into a single massive waterway.

10:57.120 --> 11:01.600
From the perspective of a boat launched a mile downstream of that confluence,

11:01.820 --> 11:06.080
the origin of the water beneath the hull is fundamentally inaccessible.

11:06.080 --> 11:10.700
And it's not simply that the information is obscured or difficult to measure.

11:11.140 --> 11:15.780
It is mathematically irrelevant to any physical interaction the boat will experience moving forward.

11:16.280 --> 11:19.780
The history of the water has no bearing on the future trajectory of the boat.

11:19.940 --> 11:20.160
Right.

11:20.560 --> 11:25.940
And to formalize this merging, Flyxion defines an equivalence relation called a continuation quotient.

11:26.640 --> 11:28.720
Two different histories are mathematically equivalent.

11:28.880 --> 11:32.760
They are placed into the same quotient if their continuation spaces are identical.

11:32.760 --> 11:39.520
And a continuation space is the formal set of all possible futures a system can reach within a specific time horizon.

11:39.800 --> 11:43.940
So if history A allows you to eventually reach states X, Y, and Z,

11:44.500 --> 11:48.560
and history B allows you to eventually reach those exact same states X, Y, and Z,

11:49.240 --> 11:54.540
then under this geometric framework, history A and history B are identical.

11:54.800 --> 11:57.380
See, my initial reaction to this was really defensive.

11:57.900 --> 12:00.840
Because our histories feel intrinsically valuable to us.

12:00.840 --> 12:07.780
The idea that two radically different paths could be deemed mathematically identical just because they arrive at the same waiting room,

12:08.380 --> 12:09.920
it feels like an erasure of the past.

12:10.100 --> 12:11.680
And that discomfort is totally natural.

12:11.900 --> 12:11.960
Yeah.

12:12.320 --> 12:19.280
But the author relies on a property called time-homogenous dynamics to prove a really important structural lemma.

12:19.620 --> 12:21.660
Under these conditions, collisions are permanent.

12:21.660 --> 12:28.340
Meaning, if two states have the exact same immediate one-step future, they will share every subsequent future forever.

12:28.460 --> 12:32.140
Because the rules governing the system don't arbitrarily change based on the clock.

12:32.460 --> 12:37.080
If state A and state B both transition into state C at the next tick of the clock,

12:37.360 --> 12:41.120
they are now both sitting in state C, subject to the exact same homogenous rules.

12:41.260 --> 12:46.020
There is literally nothing left in the mechanics of the universe to distinguish them moving forward.

12:46.400 --> 12:48.060
The collision cannot be undone.

12:48.060 --> 12:54.000
To ground this in engineering, because the math gets pretty abstract, the draft dissects a traffic light system.

12:54.380 --> 12:58.360
And it's a beautifully contained example of mechanical memory and reachable futures.

12:58.620 --> 12:59.200
It really is.

12:59.240 --> 13:03.180
Imagine an intersection controlled by a light with four distinct phases.

13:03.900 --> 13:08.120
Phase 0 is a red light for cars, opening the crosswalk for pedestrians.

13:08.940 --> 13:13.060
Phases 1, 2, and 3 cycle through various green and yellow lights for the vehicles.

13:13.060 --> 13:15.880
And alongside the phase, the system maintains a request bit.

13:16.280 --> 13:18.640
It's a mechanical memory state of either 0 or 1.

13:19.260 --> 13:21.260
This bit tracks pedestrian intent.

13:21.680 --> 13:24.400
If someone pushes the crossing button, the bit flips to 1.

13:24.640 --> 13:26.820
If no one is waiting, it remains 0.

13:27.280 --> 13:31.880
So, an engineer writing the software for this controller has to make a highly specific decision.

13:32.500 --> 13:35.900
When exactly should the system clear that request bit back to 0?

13:36.280 --> 13:40.380
When should the machine mathematically forget that a pedestrian pushed the button?

13:40.380 --> 13:45.800
And the draft contrasts two designs to illustrate the power of continuation quotients.

13:46.620 --> 13:48.620
The first is mathematically exact.

13:49.300 --> 13:54.340
The rule states, clear the request bit unconditionally upon arrival at phase 0.

13:54.660 --> 13:57.100
Okay, let's track the futures of that exact design.

13:57.880 --> 14:01.280
The light is currently at phase 3, the final yellow light before red.

14:01.820 --> 14:05.400
A pedestrian has pushed the button, so the request bit is holding a 1.

14:05.780 --> 14:06.100
Right.

14:06.340 --> 14:07.780
We will call that history A.

14:07.780 --> 14:12.920
Now, consider history B. The light is at phase 3, but the request bit is 0.

14:13.180 --> 14:14.080
No one pushed the button.

14:14.440 --> 14:19.020
Under the exact design, the transition from phase 3 dictates that the light moves to phase 0.

14:19.240 --> 14:23.140
The moment it arrives at phase 0, the software forcibly sets the bit to 0

14:23.140 --> 14:26.360
because the crosswalk is now open and the pedestrian is being served.

14:26.620 --> 14:28.880
Notice what happened to the continuation spaces there.

14:29.620 --> 14:32.460
History A and history B had completely different pasts.

14:32.460 --> 14:34.760
One had waiting pedestrian, one did not.

14:35.080 --> 14:37.420
But their one-step future is completely identical.

14:37.980 --> 14:40.040
They both arrive at phase 0 with a cleared bit.

14:40.200 --> 14:40.520
Ah.

14:41.280 --> 14:42.600
The reverse hit the confluence.

14:43.260 --> 14:47.660
The system correctly recognized that the past history of the button push during phase 3

14:47.660 --> 14:52.360
is now mathematically irrelevant because the pedestrian is currently walking across the street.

14:52.860 --> 14:53.860
It merged the histories.

14:53.860 --> 14:56.500
Flyxion calls this design exactness.

14:57.100 --> 15:02.120
The mechanical memory of the system perfectly aligns with the actual geometry of its reachable futures.

15:02.620 --> 15:10.340
But the true power of this framework reveals itself when we examine what happens when an engineer fails to achieve design exactness.

15:10.700 --> 15:12.980
This moves us into the third phase of the draft.

15:13.580 --> 15:15.580
Distinction loss under quotients.

15:15.840 --> 15:16.120
Right.

15:16.220 --> 15:20.540
Like, what is the mathematical penalty for forcing a merge too early?

15:20.540 --> 15:21.140
Exactly.

15:21.940 --> 15:27.240
If the continuation quotient is the perfect merge, an inexact quotient is a destructive merge.

15:27.960 --> 15:30.380
And the text uses a library catalog to illustrate this.

15:30.460 --> 15:31.640
A catalog is a quotient, right?

15:31.720 --> 15:37.120
It takes a thousand-page physical object and compresses it into a title, an author, and a Dewey decimal number.

15:37.360 --> 15:44.560
And a perfectly designed catalog loses no information that a patron actually requires to make a decision about retrieving the book.

15:44.720 --> 15:49.600
But imagine a poorly designed catalog that only allows two categories for the entire building.

15:49.600 --> 15:51.260
Fiction and nonfiction.

15:51.380 --> 15:52.640
Oh, that would be a nightmare.

15:53.020 --> 15:53.240
Right.

15:53.820 --> 16:05.420
You are taking a deeply personal, harrowing memoir about surviving a war and a dense academic textbook on cellular biology and forcing them into the exact same mathematical fiber.

16:06.180 --> 16:07.940
You haven't lied, they are both nonfiction.

16:08.560 --> 16:12.660
But you have destroyed distinctions that dictate entirely different futures for the patron.

16:12.840 --> 16:18.520
Because if I need to study for a biology exam, merging those two books into one category fails me entirely.

16:18.520 --> 16:22.100
And the critical contribution of this chapter is quantifying that failure.

16:23.040 --> 16:26.800
How much information is destroyed when a system forces a false merge?

16:27.480 --> 16:29.680
The draft provides a strict formula for this.

16:30.180 --> 16:37.560
The loss at any given fiber is the base two logarithm of the number of distinct continuation spaces that are forced into that single fiber.

16:37.800 --> 16:39.740
Okay, let's break down the mechanics of that formula.

16:40.080 --> 16:40.340
Sure.

16:40.700 --> 16:43.660
We look at the single bin, we shove things into our nonfiction category.

16:43.660 --> 16:49.300
We count how many genuinely different futures or distinct continuation spaces we threw in there.

16:50.000 --> 16:54.880
If we threw in textbooks, memoirs, cookbooks, and travel guides, that's four distinct futures.

16:55.100 --> 16:57.200
We take the base two logarithm of four.

16:57.360 --> 16:58.040
Which is two.

16:58.160 --> 16:59.940
Exactly two bits of information lost.

17:00.060 --> 17:00.280
Uh-huh.

17:00.520 --> 17:04.880
The logarithm scales the count of futures into a precise measure of destroyed data.

17:05.400 --> 17:10.820
If the catalog is perfectly designed, every item in a bin points to only one shared future.

17:11.440 --> 17:13.200
The number of distinct spaces is one.

17:13.200 --> 17:15.380
And the logarithm of one is zero.

17:15.840 --> 17:16.420
Zero loss.

17:16.520 --> 17:17.080
Zero loss.

17:17.520 --> 17:21.200
That realization fundamentally shifted how I viewed these systems.

17:22.000 --> 17:28.740
The continuation quotient, the perfect merging of the rivers, is actually the ultimate lossless compression algorithm.

17:29.360 --> 17:36.600
It is the absolute coarsest summary of a history you can possibly create without losing a single pixel of resolution regarding the future.

17:36.780 --> 17:37.700
That's a great way to put it.

17:37.760 --> 17:42.040
It strips away every irrelevant detail of the past while perfectly preserving the map of tomorrow.

17:42.040 --> 17:50.200
In theorem 3.1 in the text, mathematically proves that the true continuation quotient is the unique courses quotient with zero loss.

17:50.680 --> 17:54.720
Any attempt to compress the system further will result in strictly positive loss.

17:54.720 --> 18:01.240
So to see that positive loss in action, we return to the traffic light and look at the flawed alternative design.

18:02.080 --> 18:05.460
The exact design cleared the bit upon arrival at the red light.

18:05.820 --> 18:11.420
But the flawed rule states, clear the request bit whenever the system is currently sitting at phase zero.

18:11.420 --> 18:13.820
Superficially, it sounds identical, doesn't it?

18:13.940 --> 18:15.600
If the light is red, clear the bit.

18:16.000 --> 18:18.640
But the geometry of the futures tells a very different story.

18:18.940 --> 18:21.140
So imagine the light is already resting at phase zero.

18:21.500 --> 18:22.540
The crosswalk is open.

18:22.900 --> 18:24.980
A pedestrian runs up late and hits the button.

18:25.380 --> 18:26.460
The bit flips to one.

18:26.820 --> 18:27.700
That is state A.

18:28.120 --> 18:31.500
Now imagine the light is resting at phase zero and no one is there.

18:31.700 --> 18:32.780
The bit is zero.

18:33.120 --> 18:34.000
That is state B.

18:34.320 --> 18:38.080
Under the flawed design, the software evaluates its current state.

18:38.480 --> 18:39.720
It sees it is at phase zero.

18:40.120 --> 18:41.500
The rule demands it clear the bit.

18:41.880 --> 18:44.120
It forcibly changes state A into state B.

18:44.300 --> 18:44.840
It merges them.

18:45.180 --> 18:48.580
But those two states absolutely do not share the same one-step future.

18:48.780 --> 18:49.320
No, they don't.

18:49.320 --> 18:53.560
The light is about to transition out of phase zero into the green light for cars.

18:53.880 --> 18:54.380
Phase one.

18:55.080 --> 18:59.440
If no one pushed the button, you know, state B, the light should just cycle normally.

18:59.560 --> 19:02.980
But if someone just ran up and hit the button while the light was red, state A,

19:02.980 --> 19:07.820
the system must remember that intent so it can prioritize the crosswalk on the next cycle.

19:08.260 --> 19:10.420
By clearing the bit while sitting at the red light,

19:11.000 --> 19:14.600
the system effectively deleted the existence of the late-arriving pedestrian.

19:15.540 --> 19:19.280
It merged two states that possess genuinely different continuation spaces.

19:20.060 --> 19:26.100
A controller looking even one horizon ahead desperately needs to distinguish between those two states.

19:26.200 --> 19:29.740
And we can calculate the exact cost of that bad engineering, right?

19:29.740 --> 19:33.180
We force two distinct continuation spaces into a single state.

19:33.460 --> 19:36.440
The formula demands the base two logarithm of two.

19:36.700 --> 19:37.760
Which is exactly one.

19:38.160 --> 19:42.440
The mathematics prove that this specific, seemingly minor software design error

19:42.440 --> 19:45.380
caused precisely one bit of information loss.

19:45.800 --> 19:48.880
It's a beautifully rigorous way to measure systemic badness.

19:48.880 --> 19:55.820
And the draft takes this a step further by introducing eventual periodicity and the concept of the deadline.

19:56.240 --> 20:00.100
Because the request bit in that traffic system isn't universally redundant,

20:00.520 --> 20:02.380
but it isn't universally essential either.

20:02.820 --> 20:05.240
It operates on a strict expiration schedule.

20:05.600 --> 20:05.800
Right.

20:05.980 --> 20:08.800
The traffic light operates on a rigid four-step cycle.

20:08.800 --> 20:23.700
So, if the light is currently at phase one, the green light,

20:23.860 --> 20:27.520
the system inherently knows that in exactly three steps it will hit phase zero.

20:27.720 --> 20:29.860
And at phase zero, the bit will be cleared unconditionally.

20:30.500 --> 20:34.640
Therefore, the question of whether the bit is currently holding a one or zero at phase one

20:34.640 --> 20:37.420
will categorically not matter three horizons from now.

20:37.420 --> 20:41.480
The system's own structural periodicity answers the question on a delay.

20:42.140 --> 20:43.620
The memory naturally expires.

20:44.040 --> 20:48.440
And the exact design of the traffic light clears the bit at the absolute earliest possible moment

20:48.440 --> 20:53.020
after it stops mattering, which simultaneously happens to be the latest possible moment

20:53.020 --> 20:56.200
where failing to clear it would result in an observable failure.

20:56.400 --> 20:58.600
It perfectly rides the boundary of necessity.

20:58.960 --> 21:02.960
Translating this deadline concept to cognitive load is fascinating.

21:03.500 --> 21:05.680
We all carry mental request bits.

21:05.680 --> 21:06.920
We track details.

21:07.100 --> 21:10.340
We agonize over past decisions and maintain distinctions in our memory.

21:10.820 --> 21:15.900
We spend massive amounts of cognitive energy differentiating between choice A and choice B.

21:16.020 --> 21:16.800
Oh, constantly.

21:17.040 --> 21:21.180
But if the larger system we are operating with in our workplace dynamics, our family routines,

21:21.820 --> 21:26.780
the macroeconomic cycle is structurally guaranteed to resolve that distinction anyway in a few horizons.

21:27.020 --> 21:30.960
We are actively maintaining memory that has already lost its continuation value.

21:30.960 --> 21:39.520
We endure a high cognitive carrying cost to preserve distinctions that the underlying mathematics of our lives have already scheduled for deletion.

21:40.180 --> 21:45.980
The universe has already merged the rivers, but we are still rowing against the current, insisting the water is different.

21:46.180 --> 21:49.620
We are voluntarily paying that one-bit loss of mental energy.

21:49.940 --> 21:51.540
It's an exhausting way to live.

21:51.540 --> 21:58.720
Which brings us to what I consider the absolute masterstroke of this draft, phase four, the grand unification.

21:59.340 --> 22:04.320
Because up to this point, reading Flyxion's work induced a bit of conceptual whiplash.

22:04.460 --> 22:09.640
Yeah, the transition from the first chapter to the second and third represents a massive shift in mathematical philosophy.

22:09.640 --> 22:12.600
Chapter one was entirely about the verbs of creation.

22:12.960 --> 22:22.360
It was about operators generating detail, pulling shakes into self-similar fractal attractors, taking a blank space, and growing a highly complex future.

22:22.760 --> 22:26.040
And then chapters two and three were about the mathematics of destruction.

22:26.760 --> 22:35.760
They were about quotients, collapsing distinctions, forcing histories to merge, and calculating exactly how much information dies in the process.

22:35.760 --> 22:40.000
It felt like trying to reconcile general relativity with quantum mechanics.

22:40.380 --> 22:42.720
One builds the cosmos, the other breaks it down.

22:43.020 --> 22:44.600
It's a huge contradiction.

22:45.180 --> 22:53.420
How do you house a theory of infinite detail generation and a theory of merciless information compression under the exact same geometric roof?

22:53.740 --> 22:56.800
Well, Flyxion solves this by revealing they are not opposites.

22:56.940 --> 23:01.500
They are the exact same mathematical object behaving differently based on a single parameter.

23:02.380 --> 23:04.260
They are all continuation operators.

23:04.260 --> 23:07.760
Technically, they are Lipschitz maps acting on metric spaces.

23:08.300 --> 23:15.700
If we return to our magical map shrinking machine from the first chapter, the Lipschitz map is the formal mathematical generalization of that machine.

23:15.920 --> 23:22.720
It's like a rule that transforms a space, but it comes with a built-in slider, a number called the Lipschitz constant, denoted by the letter L.

23:23.280 --> 23:30.020
This constant dictates the maximum amount the operator is allowed to stretch or shrink the distances between points in that space.

23:30.020 --> 23:36.720
And if you adjust the slider so that L is strictly less than 1, the machine acts as a contraction.

23:37.460 --> 23:39.360
It relentlessly shrinks distances.

23:39.940 --> 23:41.480
And that is the entirety of chapter 1.

23:41.980 --> 23:51.520
When L is less than 1, the operator pulls everything together, relentlessly compressing the space until it generates that unique, infinitely detailed, self-similar fractal.

23:51.960 --> 23:53.320
It builds the cantor set.

23:53.320 --> 23:56.560
But the unification occurs when you slide L upwards.

23:57.040 --> 24:01.080
What happens when the Lipschitz constant is exactly 1 or greater than 1?

24:01.280 --> 24:03.140
The operator stops shrinking things.

24:03.280 --> 24:08.100
It might shuffle them around or preserve the distances or even stretch them, but the contraction stops.

24:08.100 --> 24:20.600
The draft proves that the operator we utilized in chapters 2 and 3, the operator that simply ticks the clock forward, advancing a state by one horizon to calculate its reachable future, is fundamentally a continuation operator.

24:21.060 --> 24:23.740
But its Lipschitz constant is typically 1 or greater.

24:23.740 --> 24:26.460
Because it isn't contracting to a single shape.

24:26.840 --> 24:39.860
In a finite system like our four-phase traffic light, advancing the clock steps the state forward, inevitably reduces distinctions as things hit the confluence, merges the histories into quotients, and ultimately settles into a repeating periodic cycle.

24:40.200 --> 24:42.920
The unification is breathtaking in its elegance.

24:43.700 --> 24:47.080
Generation and collapse are not warring theories of reality.

24:47.600 --> 24:50.800
They are merely different regions of the exact same parameter space.

24:50.800 --> 24:59.300
The mathematics governing the creation of an infinite future, and the mathematics dictating the forgetting of an irrelevant past, are siblings.

25:00.100 --> 25:02.780
It is a profound unification of mathematical verbs.

25:02.960 --> 25:05.720
I love how you phrase that, a unification of verbs.

25:06.100 --> 25:12.660
The text actually points out that the earlier discussions about quotients, fibers, and house-earth distances were focused on nouns.

25:12.840 --> 25:14.180
They were the objects and measurements.

25:14.800 --> 25:16.760
The continuation operators are the verbs.

25:16.760 --> 25:22.180
They do the actual work of moving reality forward, and it turns out there is only one verb.

25:22.700 --> 25:27.640
And to ensure this singular verb behaves properly when applied to complex, merging histories,

25:28.260 --> 25:32.220
Flyxion introduces a vital mathematical safeguard called gamma compatibility.

25:32.720 --> 25:38.760
When I first read about gamma compatibility, the notation was intimidating, but the concept is really just about keeping the math honest.

25:39.140 --> 25:40.780
It prevents the operators from cheating.

25:40.780 --> 25:41.420
Exactly.

25:41.940 --> 25:50.180
If you have an operator, a rule that takes action on a system's history, it must respect the equivalence classes established by the continuation quotient.

25:50.440 --> 25:56.580
To put that in the context of our river analogy, if the two rivers have perfectly merged and a boat is floating downstream,

25:56.980 --> 26:02.520
a gamma-compatible operator is a rule that governs the boat without secretly checking where the water came from.

26:02.520 --> 26:10.300
Any action taken on a history cannot depend on information that the continuation quotient has already legally declared dead.

26:10.560 --> 26:10.780
Right.

26:11.260 --> 26:13.500
Consider a rule that violates gamma compatibility.

26:14.440 --> 26:16.620
Imagine a time-traveling riverboat operator.

26:17.240 --> 26:17.920
The rule says,

26:18.460 --> 26:25.360
If the boat is at the confluence, turn left, but only if the water beneath the hull originally came from the northern glacier.

26:25.360 --> 26:32.780
It's sneaking into the mathematical trash can, pulling out the past history that we explicitly proved had no bearing on the future,

26:33.140 --> 26:35.400
and using it to alter the trajectory of the boat.

26:35.620 --> 26:36.980
It breaks the equivalence.

26:37.420 --> 26:41.720
Gamma compatibility is the rigorous algebraic firewall that forbids that behavior.

26:41.920 --> 26:42.160
Oh.

26:42.360 --> 26:47.980
It mathematically guarantees that if two histories have equivalent futures, the operator is blind to their differences.

26:48.660 --> 26:50.400
It ensures the verb respects the noun.

26:50.600 --> 26:55.220
Armed with this unified, gamma-compatible machinery, we arrive at the fifth phase.

26:55.600 --> 26:56.680
The unsolved mystery.

26:57.400 --> 27:02.340
Because Flyxion builds this incredible mathematical engine, proves its internal consistency,

27:02.780 --> 27:05.940
and then points it directly at a staggering unsolved question.

27:06.360 --> 27:09.100
They title this section Toward Universal Generation.

27:09.640 --> 27:14.960
This is the moment the draft transitions from establishing a foundation to gazing at the horizon of the field.

27:15.560 --> 27:17.620
The central question is profoundly ambitious.

27:17.620 --> 27:25.300
Can a small, fixed, finite set of these continuation operators generate any possible future geometry?

27:25.300 --> 27:30.380
To grasp the magnitude of that question, consider how generative systems work in our daily lives.

27:30.760 --> 27:31.840
Take the English dictionary.

27:32.440 --> 27:34.920
The English alphabet consists of 26 letters.

27:35.200 --> 27:37.700
That is a very small, rigidly first set of operators.

27:37.700 --> 27:45.620
Yet, those 26 letters can generate every single word in the dictionary, every book ever written, and every word that hasn't even been invented yet.

27:45.620 --> 27:47.740
Or consider the mechanics of chess.

27:48.780 --> 27:53.980
You have a finite board of 64 squares and a very small set of allowed movements for the pieces.

27:54.700 --> 28:03.740
Yet, those rigidly fixed rules generate an effectively unbounded state space of possible games, far more games than there are atoms in the observable universe.

28:03.740 --> 28:05.740
It is the ultimate generative trick.

28:06.260 --> 28:10.780
You use a microscopic vocabulary to reach a practically infinite space of outcomes.

28:11.580 --> 28:12.580
Flyxion is asking,

28:13.320 --> 28:15.960
Can we accomplish this for the geometry of time and choice?

28:16.500 --> 28:23.360
Can we discover a core alphabet of mathematical rules that can build literally any future continuation space we could possibly imagine?

28:23.660 --> 28:30.680
To attack this monumental problem, the author deploys a highly specialized mathematical shortcut known as the collage theorem.

28:30.680 --> 28:34.500
The name evokes art, but the mechanics are pure computational bypass.

28:35.180 --> 28:41.480
Earlier, we established that a set of contracting rules eventually converges on a final shape the attractor at the absolute limit of infinity.

28:41.880 --> 28:47.200
But calculating the state of a system at the limit of infinity is computationally intractable.

28:47.200 --> 28:59.060
If you are an engineer or a mathematician trying to find a specific set of rules that will perfectly build a target shape, say, a specific highly desired future state for an economy,

28:59.620 --> 29:05.760
you cannot afford to run millions of simulated rules out to infinity just to see if they eventually land on your target.

29:05.760 --> 29:08.060
It's an impossible inverse problem.

29:08.380 --> 29:10.120
The collage theorem provides the bypass.

29:10.800 --> 29:14.280
It proves that you do not need to calculate the sequence all the way to infinity.

29:14.580 --> 29:21.500
You only need to check if one single application of the rules, a one-step collage, maps the space reasonably close to your target.

29:21.840 --> 29:22.120
Right.

29:22.440 --> 29:27.320
If you apply the rules once, and the immediate output roughly covers the shape of your target,

29:27.740 --> 29:32.720
the theorem guarantees that if you were to run those rules to infinity, they would permanently lock onto that target.

29:32.720 --> 29:39.340
It magically converts an intractable infinite limit problem into a highly manageable one-step geometric fitting problem.

29:39.640 --> 29:43.120
So with the collage theorem acting as our bypass, do we have the answer?

29:43.380 --> 29:46.000
Do we have our universal alphabet for generating futures?

29:46.640 --> 29:51.840
Well, Flyxion provides theorem 5.2, which delivers exactly half of the answer.

29:52.300 --> 29:59.980
The theorem proves that, yes, every compact target, every reasonable bounded future geometry can absolutely be approximated.

29:59.980 --> 30:03.040
A set of rules to build it definitively exists.

30:03.280 --> 30:04.880
But the caveat is devastating.

30:05.080 --> 30:07.040
It is a massive mathematical limitation.

30:07.700 --> 30:15.280
The theorem guarantees the existence of the rules, but it requires you to custom build an entirely new set of rules for every single new target.

30:15.460 --> 30:17.860
Which means we do not have a 26-letter alphabet.

30:18.200 --> 30:18.340
Yeah.

30:18.340 --> 30:26.000
We have a system where, if I want to write the word Apple, I am forced to invent five completely unique custom letters that can only spell Apple.

30:26.400 --> 30:32.000
And if I want to write Banana, I have to throw the first set away and invent a completely different set of custom letters just for that word.

30:32.060 --> 30:32.440
Exactly.

30:32.740 --> 30:37.660
The existence theorem answers the question, can this specific destination be reached at all?

30:38.060 --> 30:39.720
The answer is undeniably yes.

30:39.720 --> 30:43.420
But the universal generation conjecture asks a much harder question.

30:44.080 --> 30:48.160
Can every destination be reached using a single set of tools chosen in advance?

30:48.500 --> 30:51.920
And the instinct is to ask why this is so difficult to achieve.

30:52.480 --> 30:56.120
Artificial neural networks seemingly bypass this problem constantly.

30:56.860 --> 31:02.340
A large language model uses a fixed, pre-chosen architecture to generate virtually any text.

31:02.340 --> 31:11.280
In mathematics, the universal approximation theorem guarantees that a neural network with enough width or depth can approximate any continuous function.

31:11.800 --> 31:16.060
Why does this geometric framework fail where neural networks succeed?

31:16.380 --> 31:20.780
That gap is the exact open conjecture Flyxion leaves unsolved.

31:21.140 --> 31:24.140
It represents a genuine frontier in geometric mathematics.

31:24.640 --> 31:29.880
The reason neural networks achieve universal approximation is rooted in their compositional depth.

31:29.880 --> 31:36.660
They possess a layered structure that allows simple functions to combine in complex ways, guaranteeing coverage of the target space.

31:36.780 --> 31:38.320
It's similar to polynomials, right?

31:38.660 --> 31:42.640
We know polynomials can approximate any curve because of the Stone-Weir-Strauss theorem.

31:42.820 --> 31:44.040
They have an algebraic closure.

31:44.200 --> 31:47.000
You can multiply them together, add them up, and they behave smoothly.

31:47.400 --> 31:47.920
Precisely.

31:48.300 --> 31:51.940
Neural networks and polynomials possess proven structural closure.

31:52.560 --> 31:54.600
Continuation geometries currently do not.

31:54.600 --> 32:03.180
We know the destination exists on the map, but we lack the mathematical proof that a universal vehicle can be constructed to navigate there using a finite engine.

32:03.940 --> 32:06.660
Flyxion is remarkably transparent about this limitation.

32:07.460 --> 32:10.880
Closing that structural gap is the next great challenge for the field.

32:10.880 --> 32:19.640
There is something thrilling about reading a dense mathematical text that suddenly stops at the edge of the known map, plants a flag, and basically says,

32:20.100 --> 32:21.620
Here be dragons, good luck.

32:22.080 --> 32:26.220
Which leads us perfectly into the final phase of our deep dive, the roadmap.

32:26.720 --> 32:33.180
The working draft deliberately pauses here, but Flyxion includes an outline of the unfinished chapters they intend to write next.

32:33.520 --> 32:35.780
It reads like the private sketchbook of an inventor.

32:35.780 --> 32:40.520
It offers a rare, unsiltered glimpse into the real-time process of mathematical philosophy.

32:41.040 --> 32:44.340
The author outlines the conceptual hurdles they are preparing to tackle.

32:44.500 --> 32:48.780
Let's examine the notes for the plan, Chapter 6, titled State Insufficiency Made Explicit.

32:49.440 --> 32:52.220
The notes describe a sleep coma toy system.

32:52.620 --> 32:53.920
What is the conceptual goal there?

32:54.240 --> 32:59.740
The goal is to formally, mathematically prove a highly subtle philosophical point.

33:00.640 --> 33:05.160
Observable state is drastically insufficient to capture the true geometry of the future.

33:05.160 --> 33:10.580
The author wants to demonstrate that two systems can look completely identical to an outside observer,

33:10.860 --> 33:14.240
yet possess mathematically distinct continuation spaces.

33:14.720 --> 33:16.420
Consider the medical reality of that.

33:16.760 --> 33:18.760
Imagine two patients in a hospital ward.

33:19.420 --> 33:21.840
One patient is in a deep, natural sleep.

33:22.320 --> 33:24.480
The other patient is in a medically induced coma.

33:25.240 --> 33:29.380
If a doctor walks into the room and merely observes their current state, the data is identical.

33:29.980 --> 33:34.140
Both patients have their eyes closed, their breathing is steady, their heart rates are suppressed.

33:34.140 --> 33:37.060
Their observable internal states are indistinguishable.

33:37.260 --> 33:41.840
But their continuation spaces, their mathematically accessible futures are wildly divergent.

33:42.360 --> 33:48.240
The naturally sleeping person's one-step future includes the possibility of waking up immediately if a loud alarm sounds.

33:48.840 --> 33:54.340
The coma patient's one-step future absolutely does not include that possibility, regardless of the alarm.

33:54.340 --> 34:02.960
Flyxion wants to build a rigorous geometric proof, demonstrating that if you rely solely on observable state, you will inevitably miscalculate the future.

34:03.680 --> 34:09.960
The underlying history, the hidden rules governing the continuation space, must be integrated into the geometry.

34:09.960 --> 34:27.420
Flyxion wants to utilize this entire geometric framework of attractors, quotients, and operators to define the concept of meaning itself.

34:27.420 --> 34:35.600
It is perhaps the most ambitious note in the draft, defining meaning not as linguistic abstraction, but as an equivalence class of reachable futures.

34:35.980 --> 34:45.400
If we follow that logic, two different sentences or two different political actions possess the exact same meaning if and only if they lead to the exact same set of reachable futures.

34:46.020 --> 34:49.400
Meaning is no longer a floating concept debated by philosophers.

34:49.740 --> 34:52.620
It is literally the measurable shape of what happens next.

34:52.620 --> 34:58.520
It echoes the pragmatist philosophy of William James or Charles Pierce, but grounded in hard geometry.

34:59.060 --> 35:02.960
However, Flyxion's own notes reveal incredible restraint here.

35:03.680 --> 35:12.840
They explicitly caution themselves against inventing new mathematical symbols or jumping to grand conclusions before fully mapping the underlying structure.

35:13.100 --> 35:15.420
They ask themselves a critical question in the margins.

35:15.960 --> 35:19.760
Is meaning simply calculated using the standard Hausdorff distance?

35:19.760 --> 35:25.240
Or does it require a much more complex topological relationship like a homeomorphism?

35:25.800 --> 35:28.180
It demonstrates the immense integrity of the author.

35:28.500 --> 35:35.320
They are actively telling themselves not to write the theorem until they rigorously understand what the equal sign actually represents in that context.

35:35.840 --> 35:41.080
They refuse to dress up a philosophical speculation in the authoritative clothing of a mathematical proof.

35:41.080 --> 35:44.380
We have covered a massive amount of intellectual terrain today.

35:44.800 --> 35:55.100
We started by dismantling the idea of the future as a dark room, replacing it with the mechanics of fractal generation tiny rules acting as contractions, pulling reality into self-similar attractors.

35:55.360 --> 36:05.900
We examined the destruction of history, watching different trajectories flow across the landscape and merge into perfect continuation quotients, where the past mathematically vanishes into the confluence.

36:05.900 --> 36:15.400
We calculated the exact one-bit loss of forcing a merge too early and explored how systems naturally expire their own memories through periodic deadlines.

36:15.880 --> 36:23.460
We discovered the magic parameter, the Lipschitz constant, that mathematically unifies the act of creating the future with the act of forgetting the past.

36:23.620 --> 36:30.260
And we stood at the edge of the theoretical map, contemplating the unsolved mystery of a universal alphabet for time itself.

36:30.260 --> 36:40.400
The entire framework forces a radical reframing of how we conceptualize our trajectories, the weight of our choices, and the historical information we insist on carrying with us.

36:40.520 --> 36:44.300
Which leaves a final, deeply personal application for you to consider.

36:44.660 --> 36:50.360
We now understand that the future is a geometry we are actively generating, not a room we are walking into.

36:50.960 --> 36:52.780
Look at the rivers in your own life.

36:52.780 --> 36:59.200
Look at a choice you are currently agonizing over, a distinction you are spending massive amounts of cognitive energy to maintain.

36:59.840 --> 37:03.140
Are those two paths actually generating different futures?

37:03.820 --> 37:08.180
Or do they share the exact same continuation space just a few horizons away?

37:08.620 --> 37:15.980
Are you unnecessarily paying a daily one-bit loss in mental energy to preserve a difference that the universe has already merged?

37:16.240 --> 37:19.380
It is a geometric question with profound psychological weight.

37:19.380 --> 37:22.960
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the architecture of the future.

37:23.420 --> 37:25.860
Keep generating your geometry, and we'll see you next time.

